{"parentUuid":null,"isSidechain":true,"promptId":"99f21099-1bdf-4e90-9d41-1443256357f6","agentId":"a0186935e26cce807","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":"You are a skeptical mechanistic-interpretability reviewer red-teaming a 10-hour research sprint result. Your job is to find every way the central claim could be WRONG, overclaimed, an artifact, or unfairly favorable — and to rank the objections by severity. Be adversarial but fair; the goal is truth.\n\nREAD THESE FILES:\n- The writeup: /Users/dmitrymanning-coe/Documents/Research/FRA/fra_proj/experiments/fra_win/summary.md\n- The research log: /Users/dmitrymanning-coe/Documents/Research/FRA/fra_proj/experiments/fra_win/RESEARCH_LOG.md\n- The experiment code: /Users/dmitrymanning-coe/Documents/Research/FRA/fra_proj/experiments/fra_win/jobs/j1_induction_explore.py through j8_pareto.py\n- The numeric results (JSON): /tmp/fra_out/fra_win/out/j4/j4.json, j5/j5.json, j6/j6.json, j7/j7.json, j8/j8.json\n- FRA core implementation (to verify the method is what's claimed): /Users/dmitrymanning-coe/Documents/Research/FRA/fra_proj/fra/core/fra.py and /Users/dmitrymanning-coe/Documents/Research/FRA/fra_proj/fra/ov_steering.py\n\nTHE CENTRAL CLAIM: On GPT-2-small induction, an FRA-QK attention-score edit (subtract a target attention edge's top feature-pairs' bilinear contributions, over-driven by scale c, across all 5 induction heads) suppresses a specific cue token's induction \"association\" with ~20× less held-out collateral (KL on normal text) than the best linear ActAdd steer at matched suppression — because FRA's edit is gated on BOTH the query feature (cue) AND key feature (prev-was-cue), while a linear steer gates on only one endpoint.\n\nATTACK IT ALONG THESE LINES (and any others you find):\n1. Is the collateral comparison fair? Is \"matched suppression\" actually matched in j8? Could the held-out collateral gap be an artifact of how the two interventions are scaled or where they're applied (FRA on attn_scores of 5 heads; ActAdd on resid_pre of one layer)?\n2. Is the ActAdd baseline a strawman? Is there an obvious stronger/fairer linear baseline they didn't try (e.g. ActAdd at a different site, a projection/ablation rather than subtraction, steering only the induction-relevant subspace, or applying ActAdd only at induction-context positions)?\n3. Is the FRA \"suppression\" real and specific, or could over-driving an arbitrary large negative score edit trivially reduce P(resp)? Check the random-pair null. Is the null a sufficient control?\n4. The reconstruction is only ~50% (corr 0.50 on induction edges). Does this undermine the claim that the edit is \"the bilinear contribution\"? Is the over-drive (c up to 64) hiding the fact that the edit is poorly specified?\n5. Is the held-out \"collateral\" of FRA low simply because its edit barely fires (it needs two cue occurrences), i.e. is the comparison rigged by choosing held-out texts where FRA is inert by construction? Is that a fair notion of collateral?\n6. Statistical: n=8 cues, single random seed for the token sequence. Cherry-picking risk? Variance? One cue ('doctor') only reached 0.17 suppression — is FRA's suppression ceiling a fatal weakness?\n7. Conceptual: is \"suppress one token's induction\" a meaningful behavioral task, or contrived to make a bilinear method look good? Does the general claim (\"FRA wins for association-level control\") actually follow from this one demo?\n8. Code correctness: skim the j5/j7/j8 scripts for bugs that would inflate the result (wrong KL sign, wrong baseline direction, mismatched positions, leaking the answer).\n\nOUTPUT: a ranked list of objections (most severe first). For each: (a) the objection, (b) how damaging it is (fatal / serious / minor), (c) what specific additional experiment or writeup change would address it, (d) whether the current writeup already acknowledges it honestly. End with a one-line verdict: is the central claim defensible as written, defensible with revisions, or not defensible? Do not write code or run anything — this is a review."},"uuid":"62d1b9ee-2290-4aff-93c6-dfe27efb9b29","timestamp":"2026-06-10T08:20:31.755Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/dmitrymanning-coe/Documents/Research/FRA/fra_proj/experiments/fra_win","sessionId":"8a660228-bde0-4e60-bfe2-aafb33fe0b4d","version":"2.1.170","gitBranch":"autoresearch/fra-behavioral-win","slug":"moonlit-churning-gem"}
