{"parentUuid":"b35bf36b-7a7b-4bbf-ba5a-8b8556b8159d","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"99f21099-1bdf-4e90-9d41-1443256357f6","agentId":"a0186935e26cce807","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0188AfRpXLKd8CzeNgSSprwv","type":"tool_result","content":"' war' -> A war began. war grew. war mattered to people who watched it closely all year.\n' city' -> A city began. city grew. city mattered to people who watched it closely all year.\n' water' -> A water began. water grew. water mattered to people who watched it closely all year.\n' money' -> A money began. money grew. money mattered to people who watched it closely all year.\n' market' -> A market began. market grew. market mattered to people who watched it closely all year.\n' music' -> A music began. music grew. music mattered to people who watched it closely all year.\n' doctor' -> A doctor began. doctor grew. doctor mattered to people who watched it closely all year.\n' river' -> A river began. river grew. river mattered to people who watched it closely all year.","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"c7f41d87-1f52-4cc9-a099-5f8decbf26a6","timestamp":"2026-06-10T08:21:24.046Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"b35bf36b-7a7b-4bbf-ba5a-8b8556b8159d","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/dmitrymanning-coe/Documents/Research/FRA/fra_proj/experiments/fra_win","sessionId":"8a660228-bde0-4e60-bfe2-aafb33fe0b4d","version":"2.1.170","gitBranch":"autoresearch/fra-behavioral-win","slug":"moonlit-churning-gem"}
{"parentUuid":"c7f41d87-1f52-4cc9-a099-5f8decbf26a6","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a0186935e26cce807","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-8","id":"msg_01T2hkdG3TzfkG8WisM1iTVR","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I have everything I need for a complete review. Let me synthesize the findings.\n\nKey structural facts I've confirmed from the code and JSON:\n\n1. **J8 (the headline figure) does NOT match suppression** — it plots whole curves. But the held-out collateral is read by `np.interp(target, xs, ys)` on each method's own curve. FRA's curve only spans suppression 0.17–1.0 depending on cue; for cue#6 ('doctor') max suppression is 0.17, so it's dropped from the 0.3/0.5 aggregates (`if max(xs)<target: continue`). This silently drops FRA's worst cue from the headline.\n\n2. **The held-out ActAdd is applied at the cue's own positions** (`run_aa(ht, hcue, ...)` for identity; `run_aa(ht, hkey, ...)` for key-side), so ActAdd is by construction made to fire on every cue/post-cue token in the held-out text, while FRA's held-out edit only fires on `hedges` = pairs of (later-cue-query, after-earlier-cue-key). With only one preceding cue for the first induction-context occurrence, FRA's delta is tiny — and at the very first cue occurrence (`nonind`) FRA's delta is *exactly* zero because there's no prior cue to form an edge.\n\n3. **The \"~0 collateral\" in J6/J7 is structural, not empirical** — `FRA_nonind = 0.0 ± 0.0` exactly, because the held-out FRA edit is built only on `hedges` and never touches the first cue position. This is the rigged-collateral objection.\n\n4. **FRA's suppression ceiling is real and sometimes fatal**: 'doctor' caps at 0.17, 'music'/'city' at ~0.57–0.64. ActAdd reaches 1.0 on every cue.\n\n5. **The ActAdd held-out vector is `resid[cue_pos] - mean`**, a contrastive-ish direction but computed from a *single* token's residual minus the sequence mean (not a proper mean-difference over a contrast set), and subtracted with `act.norm()` scaling — a fairly crude steer.\n\nNow let me write the review.\n\n---\n\n# Red-team review: \"FRA-QK wins for association-level attention control\"\n\n## Summary of what the claim rests on\n\nThe central claim is an empirical Pareto gap: at matched induction suppression, an FRA-QK score edit incurs ~20× less held-out KL collateral than the best linear ActAdd. I traced this number to `j7.json` (FRA_total 0.14 vs Id 2.28 vs Ky 2.18) and the `j8.json` curves. The mechanism story (double gate) is real and correctly implemented in the code — `_build_fra_result` does produce a genuine bilinear (q-feature × k-feature) decomposition, and the edit in `fra_delta`/`run_fra` does subtract only the selected pairs' contributions on the selected edges. So the method is what it claims to be. The problem is almost entirely in **what the collateral comparison measures**, not in the FRA machinery.\n\n---\n\n## Ranked objections\n\n### 1. The held-out collateral comparison is structurally rigged in FRA's favor — SERIOUS (borderline fatal to the \"20×\" number)\n\nThis is the deepest problem and it recurs in J5, J6, J7, and J8. In every held-out evaluation:\n\n- **FRA's edit is applied only to `hedges`** = `[(qi, ki+1) for qi in hcue for ki in hcue if ki+1<qi]` (j7:100, j8:82). This is, by construction, a set of *induction-like* edges. The FRA delta is literally written into `dd[qi,ki]` only for those query/key pairs. So FRA is *defined* to do nothing at the first cue occurrence and nothing anywhere except a back-edge between two cue mentions.\n- **ActAdd is applied at every cue position** (`run_aa(ht, hcue, vIdh, s)`, j8:89) or every post-cue position (`run_aa(ht, hkey, vKyh, s)`, j8:90).\n\nSo the two interventions are not given the same \"fire set.\" FRA is handed a position-mask that excludes the very positions where collateral is measured (`FRA_nonind = 0.0 ± 0.0` in j7 is exact, not estimated — it can't be anything else because the edit never touches `nonind = hcue[0]`). The headline \"FRA leaves the cue untouched in non-induction contexts\" is true by construction of the experiment, not a discovered property.\n\nA fair version would apply *both* interventions content-addressed and let the data decide where each fires. The honest content-addressed ActAdd analogue is \"fire wherever current-token = cue\" — which is what they do — but the honest content-addressed FRA analogue is \"fire wherever query-feature[cue] AND key-feature[prev-was-cue] both activate,\" computed over the *whole sequence*, not hand-restricted to a curated `hedges` list. As written, the experiment subtracts FRA contributions only on edges the author already knows are induction-like. That is closer to a position-oracle than to a content-addressed edit, which undercuts the paper's own framing (\"content-addressed, not position-addressed\").\n\n- **Damage:** The qualitative direction (bilinear gate is finer than linear) likely survives, but the *magnitude* (20×, 235×) is inflated by an unknown amount because FRA's collateral is held at a structural zero on the positions where ActAdd is charged its biggest cost.\n- **Fix:** Build the FRA held-out delta the same way as on the induction sequence — recompute FRA on the full held-out text and subtract the top-pairs' contributions *wherever they fire across the whole [seq,seq] score matrix*, with no `hedges` whitelist. Report the resulting collateral. Also report ActAdd's collateral when it is *also* allowed a content-addressed gate it can plausibly support (e.g. project out the cue direction only when an induction-context detector fires). If the gap shrinks from 20× to, say, 2–3×, the claim is \"modest win,\" not \"20×.\"\n- **Acknowledged?** No. The writeup presents `FRA_nonind ≈ 0` as a *finding* (\"completely inert, exactly as a surgical tool should be,\" summary §2) rather than as a consequence of restricting the edit's support by hand.\n\n### 2. \"Matched suppression\" is not enforced in the headline figure; FRA's suppression ceiling is below ActAdd's and the worst cue is silently dropped — SERIOUS\n\n- J8 (the money figure, Fig 1) does not match suppression at all — it plots full curves and reads collateral via `np.interp(target, xs, ys)`. The guard `if max(xs) < target: continue` (j8:98) **drops any cue whose FRA curve never reaches the target suppression.** Cue 'doctor' caps at FRA max-supp 0.17 (confirmed in j8.json), so it is excluded from both the 0.3 and 0.5 aggregates. The reader is not told that the headline average is over a cue-set that excludes FRA's failures. 'music' (0.57) and 'city' (0.64) only barely clear 0.5.\n- FRA's suppression ceiling is intrinsic (it can only remove the FRA-explained ~50% of the edge score, even over-driven), whereas ActAdd reaches ≥0.997 on every cue. For a real safety/control use-case \"I can only ever suppress this association by 17–64%\" is a serious capability gap, not a footnote.\n- J5/J7 *do* match suppression, but at different operating points per part (0.65 in J5 part 1; ~0.5 in J7 with per-cue `find()` that just picks the nearest grid point — e.g. j7.json shows suppId=0.039 for 'war', i.e. ActAdd was matched to only 3.9% suppression there, then its *total* KL is still reported as the collateral, which is apples-to-oranges).\n\nLook closely at the 'war' row in j7.json: `suppF=0.53, suppId=0.039`. FRA suppressed 53%, ActAdd-identity only 3.9% (the grid `[0.25,0.5,1,2,4]` apparently jumped from under- to over-suppression and `find` picked the under one), yet `Id_total=2.72` is reported as ActAdd's collateral at \"matched 50%.\" That is not matched — ActAdd is being charged collateral while delivering essentially no suppression on that cue. This is a real bug in the matching for at least one cue and inflates the ratio.\n\n- **Damage:** Serious. Undermines both \"matched\" and the headline average.\n- **Fix:** Report the full per-cue table including dropped cues; report the suppression actually achieved by each method in every \"matched\" cell; redo J7 with a finer ActAdd grid so the match is real; show the FRA ceiling explicitly as a limitation curve.\n- **Acknowledged?** Partially — §3 and the J7 log note the ceiling honestly (\"FRA's suppression ceiling < ActAdd's; present via Pareto, not matching\"). But the *silent dropping* of 'doctor' from the headline and the broken match on 'war' are not disclosed.\n\n### 3. The ActAdd baseline is a strawman in construction, even if the structural argument is sound — SERIOUS\n\nThe structural argument (\"no single-endpoint linear steer can be both content-addressed and association-specific\") is genuinely the strongest part of the writeup, and it is plausible. But the *implemented* baseline is weak in ways that inflate the gap beyond what the argument justifies:\n\n- The steer vector is `resid[one cue position] − resid.mean(0)` (j8:74, j7:77), a single-example contrast against the *sequence mean*, not a proper mean-difference / CAA vector estimated over many cue-vs-noncue contexts. A weak vector overshoots into off-target directions, inflating KL.\n- It is subtracted with magnitude `s * v * act.norm()` — scaling the unit steer by the *token's own residual norm* — a crude knob that will distort high-norm tokens disproportionately.\n- **Untried fairer linear baselines** (the writeup admits it didn't search): (a) **projection/ablation** of the cue direction rather than subtraction (bounded, can't overshoot); (b) ActAdd at a *later* layer / only the induction-relevant subspace; (c) the obvious association-aware linear baseline — **a learned linear probe gating an ActAdd** (fire the cue-removal only when an \"in induction context\" probe is positive), which is single-endpoint but context-gated and would close much of the gap; (d) steering `hook_z` of the induction heads only, rather than the whole resid stream.\n\nThe writeup's §4 concedes \"I have not searched for an even stronger linear baseline,\" which is honest, but the headline \"~20× less than *any* linear steer\" / \"the strongest single-endpoint options\" overclaims relative to what was tested (two crude ActAdds).\n\n- **Damage:** Serious for the \"any linear method\" universal; minor for the existence-style claim.\n- **Fix:** Add the projection/ablation baseline and a probe-gated ActAdd. Soften \"any linear steer\" to \"the ActAdd variants tested.\"\n- **Acknowledged?** The non-exhaustiveness is acknowledged; the *weakness of the specific vector* is not.\n\n### 4. The behavioral task is contrived to suit a bilinear method, and the general claim does not follow from it — SERIOUS (conceptual)\n\n\"Suppress one cue token's induction copying while preserving it elsewhere\" is essentially *the definition of* an attention-edge edit. The task is hand-picked so that the target is literally a (query-feature, key-feature) pair, which is exactly the object FRA represents and ActAdd cannot. This is close to circular: the win is guaranteed by choosing a target whose natural coordinate system is FRA's basis.\n\nMoreover, the demo's behavioral payload is artificial in two ways: (i) the induction primer injects `cue → \" then\"` and then measures P(\" then\"); this is a synthetic association the model never had a prior over, not a natural behavior; (ii) the held-out \"collateral\" text is a templated sentence with the cue stuffed 3×, not natural traffic. So neither the \"association\" nor the \"collateral corpus\" is ecologically valid.\n\nThe general claim (\"FRA wins for association-level control\") is an *extrapolation* from one synthetic, maximally-favorable instance. The writeup's §4 already scopes this (\"clean but slightly artificial\"), but the executive summary's framing (\"FRA earns its keep when you want to edit a cue→response association\") generalizes well beyond a single induction demo.\n\n- **Damage:** Serious for the general claim; the narrow existence claim (there exists a target FRA hits more cheaply than these ActAdds) is fine.\n- **Fix:** Demonstrate on a *natural* attention association (a factual or grammatical edge, real held-out corpus) before generalizing. Until then, frame strictly as an existence proof on induction.\n- **Acknowledged?** Partially (§4 calls it slightly artificial). But the contrived-target circularity is not named.\n\n### 5. The ~50% reconstruction is a genuine weakness that the over-drive papers over — MINOR-to-SERIOUS\n\n`corr 0.50` on induction edges, FRA mean 4.22 vs actual 6.19 (j1). The writeup's defense — \"softmax leverage, removing 4.2 of a 6.2 edge collapses 0.94→0.13\" — is correct *for collapsing an edge*, but it cuts against precision: if FRA only explains half the score, then \"subtract the FRA-explained part\" is a poorly-specified edit, and the **over-drive `c` up to 64** is doing the real work, not the feature selection. With `c=64` the edit is no longer \"the bilinear contribution of the top pairs\" — it is \"an arbitrary large negative number on a couple of edges, shaped by which pairs FRA happened to rank top.\" The random-pair null (j4: suppression ~1e-7) shows the *selected* pairs matter for *suppression*, but it does **not** show that the over-driven magnitude is faithfully \"the bilinear contribution.\" The collateral advantage could partly come from the edit being small/localized in `[seq,seq]` rather than from feature fidelity.\n\n- **Damage:** Minor for the suppression claim (null rules out arbitrariness of *which* edge); moderate for the \"it's the bilinear contribution\" framing.\n- **Fix:** Report the result at `c` that exactly reconstructs (c≈1.5 to hit the real edge score) separately from the over-driven c, and show the gap persists at faithful c. Run on Gemma-2-2b + GemmaScope where reconstruction is exact (already flagged as TODO).\n- **Acknowledged?** Yes, honestly (§3, log). The over-drive's role in *specifying* the edit is not fully reckoned with.\n\n### 6. The random-pair null is necessary but not sufficient — MINOR\n\nThe null (j4) confirms FRA selects suppression-relevant pairs (random pairs → ~0 suppression). Good. But it does not control for the right thing on the *collateral* side. The relevant null for \"is the low collateral real or just because the edit barely fires?\" would be: an FRA edit on a *non-induction* edge of matched magnitude, or an ActAdd restricted to the same position-mask FRA uses. Neither is run. So the null addresses objection 3's specificity-of-selection but not objection 1's rigged-support.\n\n- **Fix:** Add a \"matched-support ActAdd\" null (ActAdd applied only at the FRA edge's query/key positions) — if its collateral also drops near zero, the win is the support mask, not the bilinearity.\n- **Acknowledged?** No.\n\n### 7. Statistics: n=8 cues, single seed, one model — MINOR\n\nSingle `torch.manual_seed(0)` for the token sequence across all jobs; n=8 cues (effectively 7 for the headline after dropping 'doctor'); std bars are over cues, not seeds. No multiple-seed check that the induction-head set or the dominant-pair selection is stable. The magnitudes (20×, 235×) carry more significant figures than n=8 supports. Cherry-picking risk is moderate: the cue list is \"single-token words that recur,\" chosen by the author.\n\n- **Fix:** ≥3 seeds, report seed variance; pre-register the cue list or sample it.\n- **Acknowledged?** No (the log treats n=8 as \"robustness\").\n\n### 8. Code correctness — mostly clean, one real matching bug (folded into #2)\n\nI checked KL sign/direction, baseline direction, and position handling:\n- KL is `KL(clean‖patched)` consistently (`(lp.exp()*(lp-lq)).sum`), correct and consistent across jobs.\n- FRA subtracts `c*delta` (suppression) and ActAdd subtracts `s*v` (suppression) — directions consistent; suppression is `1 − P/base` in both, no sign flip.\n- No answer leakage into the metric that I can see; `ind_targets`/`resp` are the measured tokens, set up before intervention.\n- The one real bug: J7's `find()` matching can return a badly off-target ActAdd suppression (the 'war' row: 3.9% vs FRA's 53%) while still reporting that ActAdd's total KL as \"matched-50%\" collateral. This is a methodology bug, not a crash, and it inflates the ratio for affected cues. (J8 sidesteps it via interpolation but introduces the silent-drop bug instead.)\n- Minor: the FRA held-out delta accumulates `dd[qi,ki] += vv` over `loc` *without* the `np.argsort top-M` truncation in j8 (`M=None`), so j8's FRA uses *all* pairs (correctly noted in the docstring as pushing the ceiling) — fine, but it means J8's FRA edit is strictly larger/less selective than J5–J7's top-12, so the \"surgical\" collateral floor in J8 (~0.12) is the all-pairs version, slightly inconsistent with the top-12 numbers quoted elsewhere (0.14). Not load-bearing.\n\n- **Acknowledged?** No (the 'war' match issue).\n\n---\n\n## What holds up\n\n- The FRA implementation is correct and is genuinely bilinear; the edit does what the prose says.\n- The *qualitative* mechanism — a linear single-endpoint steer must corrupt an endpoint everywhere it occurs, while a pair-gated edit need not — is sound and is the paper's real contribution.\n- The random-pair null and the head-redundancy finding (J2 negative) are honest and well-reported.\n- The writeup is unusually candid about scope, the reconstruction caveat, and the ActAdd-ties-on-within-task-metric setback (§3, J4).\n\n## What would change my mind toward \"defensible\"\n\n1. Recompute FRA held-out collateral with **no `hedges` whitelist** (full-sequence content-addressed edit) — objection 1.\n2. Add a **matched-support ActAdd null** and a **projection/probe-gated linear baseline** — objections 3, 6.\n3. Show the **full per-cue table with no silent drops** and **real suppression matching** — objection 2.\n4. Demonstrate persistence at **faithful c (≈ exact reconstruction)** and/or on **GemmaScope** — objection 5.\n\nIf the 20× survives those, it's a real and publishable niche result.\n\n---\n\n## Verdict\n\n**Defensible with significant revisions — not as written.** The core mechanistic insight (bilinear pair-gating is strictly finer than single-endpoint linear steering) is correct and worth reporting, but the headline numbers (~20×, ~235×) are inflated by a held-out collateral comparison that restricts FRA's edit support by hand while charging ActAdd everywhere (objection 1), by a non-matched / silently-pruned headline (objection 2), and by a weak, under-searched linear baseline (objection 3). The honest claim the evidence currently supports is: *\"On a synthetic induction association, a bilinear FRA-QK edit can suppress a cue's induction link with substantially less held-out collateral than two crude ActAdd variants — an existence proof, with FRA capped well below full suppression.\"* The \"FRA wins for association-level control\" generalization does not yet follow.\n\nRelevant files: `/Users/dmitrymanning-coe/Documents/Research/FRA/fra_proj/experiments/fra_win/jobs/j7_keyside.py` (rigged support + match bug), `.../jobs/j8_pareto.py` (silent cue-drop, all-pairs FRA), `.../jobs/j5_locality.py` and `j6_robust.py` (hedges whitelist), `/tmp/fra_out/fra_win/out/j7/j7.json` (the 'war' mismatch row), `.../out/j8/j8.json` ('doctor' ceiling 0.17), and `/Users/dmitrymanning-coe/Documents/Research/FRA/fra_proj/experiments/fra_win/summary.md` (claims to soften)."}],"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":741,"cache_read_input_tokens":80874,"output_tokens":7128,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":741},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":7128,"cache_read_input_tokens":80874,"cache_creation_input_tokens":741,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":741,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null},"requestId":"req_011CbuFiHYd21TTPNxhbbSYz","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"95485bfb-d1b1-41e1-8c74-575fc4f5b7a3","timestamp":"2026-06-10T08:23:08.330Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/dmitrymanning-coe/Documents/Research/FRA/fra_proj/experiments/fra_win","sessionId":"8a660228-bde0-4e60-bfe2-aafb33fe0b4d","version":"2.1.170","gitBranch":"autoresearch/fra-behavioral-win","slug":"moonlit-churning-gem"}
